EXPERIMENT (not a proposed fix): env-selected post-capture recovery probes

The a900 stops answering endpoint 0 once an image capture has completed. The
byte-identical vendor command `40 02 0001 / 0f100107` -- FP_ENROLL_CAPTURE's
"arm" -- is acknowledged in 12.7 ms before a capture and is still unanswered
1 s after one; ~1.07 s after that SETUP the sensor drops off the bus and
re-enumerates. Enrollment therefore never gets past its first sample.

This patch adds six independent recovery attempts, each OFF by default and
each selected by an environment variable, so that all of them share ONE build
and ONE install instead of six rebuild/reinstall cycles:

  FPC_X_DELAY_MS            wait this long after the previous capture before
                            re-arming. Doubles as the control measurement:
                            with a long delay and nothing in flight, usbmon
                            shows whether the sensor disconnects on its own or
                            only while a SETUP is outstanding.
  FPC_X_DRAIN, _MS          up to N short-timeout bulk-IN reads before
                            re-arming, in case a queued event is blocking the
                            device's EP0 handler.
  FPC_X_POSTDRAIN, _MS      the same reads, but issued immediately after the
                            image loop instead of before the next arm. This is
                            the only intervention still possible inside the
                            window: measured, EP0 stops answering 109 us after
                            the last image packet and the sensor detaches
                            1.076 s later, so no control command can help and a
                            bulk read is all that is left.
  FPC_X_POSTDELAY_MS        pause after the image before the next command, to
                            test whether EP0 recovers inside that window.
  FPC_X_REINIT              1 = re-send the init prologue (0x01, event read,
                                0x08, 0x0b read)
                            2 = full init including a fresh TLS handshake,
                                keeping the existing tee/bio
                            3 = as 2, preceded by release-interface,
                                g_usb_device_reset() and re-claim
  FPC_X_CAPTURE_TIMEOUT_MS  timeout for the enroll arm command ONLY. The
                            device dies ~72 ms after the host cancels that
                            transfer, which no run so far can distinguish from
                            a ~1.07 s firmware watchdog; raising just this one
                            timeout does distinguish them. Init keeps
                            CTRL_TIMEOUT so an absent device still fails fast.
  FPC_X_RETRIES, _MS        retry the arm command instead of failing the whole
                            operation on the first timeout.
  FPC_X_NODUP               do not advertise FP_DEVICE_FEATURE_IDENTIFY, so no
                            identify capture runs ahead of the enroll and the
                            first arm lands in the state the device answers.
                            The gate really is IDENTIFY and not
                            DUPLICATES_CHECK: fprintd 1.94.3 src/device.c,
                            fprint_device_enroll_start, keys the pre-enroll
                            identify off FP_DEVICE_FEATURE_IDENTIFY and runs it
                            even when the gallery is empty. Measured: clearing
                            DUPLICATES_CHECK alone changed nothing at all.

Three new states (FP_ENROLL_X_DELAY / _DRAIN / _REINIT) sit immediately before
FP_ENROLL_CAPTURE. fpc_read_dead_pixels() loops back to FP_ENROLL_X_DELAY
rather than FP_ENROLL_CAPTURE, so whatever clears the wedge runs before EVERY
sample, not just the first -- an enrollment needs eleven captures, so a fix
that only worked once would be no fix at all.

Safety: no bmRequestType 0x40 request is sent with a bRequest the driver does
not already issue (0x01, 0x02, 0x05, 0x06, 0x08, 0x09, 0x0b only). The drain
state only reads a bulk endpoint. g_usb_device_reset() and claim/release are
the same calls fpc_dev_init() makes at every open.

Two things the re-init path is careful about:

  - fpc_connect_tls() calls fpc_tls_buff_init()/fpc_tls_write_buff_init()
    unconditionally, so fpc_reinit_sm_run_state() releases the live buffers
    first.
  - it does NOT re-run FPC_INIT_TEE_INIT. fpc_tee_begin_enroll() ran back in
    FP_ENROLL_BEGIN and the accumulator is entirely host-side in libfpcbep.so
    (measured: zero USB traffic between the last image record and the arm
    command), so re-creating the tee/bio would silently discard every sample
    taken so far.

fpc_write_ctrl() is refactored into fpc_write_ctrl_timeout() plus a wrapper
that passes CTRL_TIMEOUT; no existing call site changes behaviour.

With every variable unset this patch is behaviour-identical to 0001..0004.

Applies on top of merge request 396 and patches 0001-0004.
